{"qid":"Q171393","names":["Principality of Wallachia"],"title":"Principality of Wallachia","endonym":null,"capsule":"Wallachia was a Romanian principality founded traditionally by Basarab I, who established de facto independence from the Hungarian Kingdom after the Battle of Posada (1330). Situated between the Carpathians and the Danube, it was continuously pressured by Hungary, the Ottoman Empire (which made it a tributary state from the late 14th century), and later Russia. Vlad III (the Impaler) ruled briefly and ferociously in the 1450s–1470s against Ottoman encroachment; in 1859 the Danubian Principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia united under Alexandru Ioan Cuza, forming the nucleus of modern Romania.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Wallachia's borders are broadly attested through Ottoman, Hungarian, and Venetian sources, though the precise frontier with Moldavia and Transylvania fluctuated and is reconstructed from treaty texts and fiscal registers.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Wallachia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallachia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Battle of Posada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Posada"},{"label":"Wikipedia: United Principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Principalities_of_Moldavia_and_Wallachia"}],"britannica":"place/Walachia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Wallachia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Basarab I","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1310,"end":1352,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Nicolae Alexandru","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1352,"end":1